      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You're navigating the twists and turns of the ICD-10 hierarchy, and it's like trying to untangle a ball of yarn, but with more skin lesions. When the morphology is highly non-standard or disjointed, it's like encountering a group of mischievous goblins, each with their own unique characteristics.\n\nIn this case, you'll want to use the \"Other specified\" codes from category L70, which include conditions like \"unspecified skin thickening\" or \"unspecified skin atrophy.\" However, since the morphology is highly non-standard, you may need to use the \"Unspecified\" code from category L70, which is like a \"wild card\" that can be used when the specific characteristics of the skin presentation don't fit into any other category.\n\nAnother approach is to use the \"Multiple\" codes from category L70, which can be used when there are multiple skin lesions or characteristics that don't fit into a single category. This is like trying to herd a group of goblins, each with their own unique abilities and strengths.\n\nIt's also worth noting that the ICD-10 guidelines recommend using the \"Unspecified\" code when the morphology is highly non-standard or disjointed, as it allows for",
